+## 14 October task-intake workflow correction
+
+Jörn explicitly rejected agent-process “should” questions, shifting workflow diagnosis to him, repeated narration instead of action, and ongoing resource expenditure without a useful result. Root thread `01a139dc-90cc-7c40-87bc-568d49059ffa` demonstrated these failures even after reading the skill. Its first analysis covered responses to corrections while omitting task intake itself. The related roots `01a139bf-2de5-7122-9402-56c7e6ceb1cb` and `01a139c9-e975-7842-a9b7-90142de43f26` supplied the salvage/handoff and intervention-discussion evidence. Exact working excerpts and reviews are disposable packets under `/tmp/msc-math/message-reviews/2026-10-14/intake-logs/`.
+
+The scoped change routes exploratory requests to this skill before commitment, assigns recoverable evidence and process choices to the agent, preserves substantive user decisions and approval boundaries, and makes the requested stop to narration explicit. These are direct user preferences and a workflow candidate, not a proven causal repair. The independent read-only workflow check distinguished intake from later execution. It does not establish reliable future application. Same-day handoff-wording replays in `meta-topics/agent-workflow/handoff-intervention-test-20261014/results.json` did not reproduce the original failure and do not validate this different change. Current-session loading and future behavior are unverified; no configuration change or broad evaluation is selected.
+
+The [session postmortem](../../../../meta-topics/agent-workflow/task-intake-postmortem-20261014/README.md) retains selected public conversation and call evidence. It classifies the session as failed and these changes as unvalidated; adding the wording did not complete the user's workflow-improvement task. Its delayed preparation also reproduced the gap between an announced next action and execution.
